<p>Two quick apps I&rsquo;m trying to write about, but I keep getting pulled away:</p>
<p><strong><a href="https://www.crucialtracks.org">Crucial Tracks</a>:</strong> You saw me testing here on the blog. Found out about this thanks to <a href="https://amerpie.lol">Lou Plummer</a>. It&rsquo;s a slow-paced social network/microblogging network where you can write about your favorite songs.</p>
<p>The service offers a quick prompt for the day (&ldquo;what song makes you think of your best friend?&rdquo; or &ldquo;a song you used to listen to growing up?&rdquo; etc.) combined with a quick search that finds the song with the artwork of the album. This is federated and free, which means it plays nice with the IndieWeb (that&rsquo;s us, if you read this on your RSS feed) and offers a bunch of other options for embedding and playing directly from streaming services.</p>
<p>I already have it <a href="https://www.crucialtracks.org/blog/publishing-with-micropub/">integrated with Micro.blog using Micropub</a>, which they also support now.</p>
<p><strong><a href="https://www.moments.im">Moments</a>:</strong> Think of <a href="https://glass.photo/">Glass</a>, but a free and less &ldquo;meant for serious photographers&rdquo; kind of platform. Not that you <em>can&rsquo;t</em> be a serious photographer on it, but it just welcomes everyone. The idea is to blog through your photos and tell a story.</p>
<p>I <a href="https://taonaw.com/2026/06/16/moments-looks-good-from-peter.html">mentioned</a> it briefly, and sadly didn&rsquo;t get the chance to get around to it (photos require more editing and playing around with than a quick post about a song), but it looks polished, and the guy behind it, Peter, reaches out to new folks to help them out at these early stages. Didn&rsquo;t reach out to Peter yet to ask about IndieWeb support, but I will try to.</p>
